WEBVTT

00:00:00.740 --> 00:00:03.160
<v Mike>Zombie Nation! I don't know why I said it like that.

00:00:03.460 --> 00:00:05.000
<v Mike>I'm gonna try that again. I was like being shaken.

00:00:05.540 --> 00:00:22.620
<v Mike>Zombie! Zombie Nation!

00:00:23.640 --> 00:00:34.160
<v Mike>1999! What appeared to be a harmless meteorite crashing in the Nevada desert has turned out to be Darkseed, an evil alien creature with horrible powers.

00:00:34.740 --> 00:00:42.800
<v Mike>And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:43.140 --> 00:00:44.960
<v Hosts>I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:45.200 --> 00:00:46.180
<v Hosts>And I'm Joe.

00:00:47.260 --> 00:00:48.760
<v Mike>I don't know if that counts.

00:00:48.880 --> 00:00:50.300
<v Mike>We still haven't had the two of you together.

00:00:50.300 --> 00:00:51.440
<v Sean>Even if you insist on it.

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:52.400
<v Mike>People aren't gonna know the difference.

00:00:52.580 --> 00:00:55.320
<v Joe>As far as they know, they don't know which one of us is talking right now.

00:00:55.800 --> 00:00:56.900
<v Mike>That's true, actually.

00:00:57.080 --> 00:01:02.000
<v Mike>Because nobody's ever taken the time in 400 plus episodes to differentiate our voices.

00:01:02.600 --> 00:01:05.340
<v Joe>Well, I'll leave it to them to figure out who I really am.

00:01:05.420 --> 00:01:06.680
<v Joe>And we can go on with the episode.

00:01:07.040 --> 00:01:10.280
<v Mike>What are you expecting out of a game that calls itself Zombie Nation?

00:01:10.280 --> 00:01:12.400
<v Sean>You know, I'm glad you asked that.

00:01:13.040 --> 00:01:20.360
<v Joe>Because I'll say like, you know, I may be expecting like a side scrolling beat-em-up sort of thing.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:27.000
<v Joe>But when I read the opening passage of this game, I was like, okay, there's like a story there told the story.

00:01:27.160 --> 00:01:28.500
<v Joe>I absorbed it pretty well.

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:32.100
<v Joe>I think I have a pretty good idea of what's good to come in this game.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:35.580
<v Joe>And boy, did the first frame of this game prove me wrong.

00:01:35.580 --> 00:01:37.060
<v Joe>I had no clue what I was in for.

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:44.020
<v Mike>Yeah, I think I was expecting something more like Zombies Ate My Neighbors on the Super Nintendo that game.

00:01:44.140 --> 00:01:48.780
<v Mike>Like, I just think like, this game, barely about zombies.

00:01:49.280 --> 00:01:53.560
<v Joe>Yeah, I mean, I guess maybe everything you are fighting is a zombie.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:55.360
<v Joe>Because they're kind of like people.

00:01:55.700 --> 00:01:57.880
<v Joe>So like, are they just like zombified?

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:00.780
<v Joe>Every enemy is zombified and like wouldn't be attacking you otherwise.

00:02:01.620 --> 00:02:03.460
<v Mike>Well, yeah, the whole nation is zombies.

00:02:03.760 --> 00:02:08.980
<v Joe>Right. So like, so they're not like, it's like, yeah, there's nothing that like looks like, oh, that's a zombie.

00:02:09.140 --> 00:02:10.100
<v Joe>It's a depiction of a zombie.

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:14.500
<v Joe>It's just like, the word zombie is just a justification of like why you're killing everybody.

00:02:14.500 --> 00:02:18.620
<v Mike>And from a plot perspective, that was a pretty long back of the box.

00:02:18.780 --> 00:02:23.300
<v Mike>But just to recap what happened there, a meteorite has crashed into the Nevada desert.

00:02:23.820 --> 00:02:25.220
<v Mike>Only it's not a meteorite.

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:32.280
<v Mike>It's an alien called Darkseed, which is a pretty cool name, except for the it's dark with a C instead of a K.

00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:34.620
<v Mike>Like, oh, yeah, yeah.

00:02:34.620 --> 00:02:35.980
<v Mike>Why? Why do that?

00:02:36.920 --> 00:02:37.860
<v Mike>It's a player?

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:45.700
<v Mike>Yeah. Anyway, so using magnetic rays, Darkseed has zombified the entire United States.

00:02:46.040 --> 00:02:56.440
<v Mike>And apparently one of the powers of zombifying things is that you can also bring inanimate objects to life because now the Statue of Liberty is a zombie as well.

00:02:56.440 --> 00:03:03.520
<v Mike>And I guess that also means that there's now a collection of NES games where the Statue of Liberty comes to life and you fight her.

00:03:03.880 --> 00:03:05.200
<v Mike>So that's interesting.

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:13.020
<v Mike>And then the last thing Darkseed does is seizes a legendary samurai sword called Shura.

00:03:13.340 --> 00:03:15.020
<v Mike>And sounds like all hope's lost.

00:03:15.160 --> 00:03:17.260
<v Mike>I mean, the entire United States is zombies.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:21.720
<v Mike>Usually it's like in these zombie things, the outbreak happens.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:28.520
<v Mike>And then we watch like the few people who, you know, Walking Dead style, like set up camps or whatever and try to fend themselves with the zombies.

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:29.940
<v Mike>This time it's already too late.

00:03:30.220 --> 00:03:31.340
<v Mike>Everyone is zombies.

00:03:31.740 --> 00:03:44.940
<v Mike>So the only force capable of stopping this is a giant, levitating, disembodied samurai head named Namakubi, which translates literally to severed head in Japanese.

00:03:45.920 --> 00:03:58.980
<v Joe>And I guess, yeah, go for it, was this made clear in the story and I missed it that you were going to be playing as severed floating head because I was just like, what the heck is this?

00:03:59.100 --> 00:04:02.780
<v Joe>And I thought at first like, oh, like this is really interesting.

00:04:03.200 --> 00:04:15.820
<v Joe>They just gave us this whole story about Darkseed taking over everything and I'm playing as the bad guy and I'm the one taking over because that's what it looks like at first until you realize that like, okay, I guess this is the hero that's coming to save us all.

00:04:15.820 --> 00:04:18.880
<v Mike>Yeah. Namakubi looks evil even as he's doing it.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:31.860
<v Mike>Cause like, think about it. Your task is essentially you came from Japan as a giant severed head, cross the Pacific ocean and start like shooting fireballs at all buildings across various cities in the United States.

00:04:31.860 --> 00:04:37.500
<v Mike>It's like, that's like, that's like, that's Japan doing that is like a reverse Godzilla, right?

00:04:37.680 --> 00:04:42.600
<v Mike>Cause like technically the atom bomb created Godzilla and then like wrecked havoc on Japan a second time.

00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:51.000
<v Mike>It's like now the severed head is coming over to America and destroying it, but it's actually helping us.

00:04:51.680 --> 00:05:03.560
<v Joe>Yeah. I, I, this was, this was definitely like threw me for a loop and, and, and definitely gave my, um, it helped delay my rage when I found out this game is a shmup.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:07.520
<v Joe>Cause I was so surprised that like my rage didn't kick in for a few minutes.

00:05:07.980 --> 00:05:11.680
<v Mike>Yeah. I feel like we didn't even talk about that, but you're, you're absolutely right.

00:05:11.820 --> 00:05:20.920
<v Mike>The, this game is a shmup and I'm not sure that like anything from the plot gave that away other than the fact that you're a floating head, but you could be a floating head in an action style game.

00:05:21.000 --> 00:05:30.020
<v Mike>But yeah, this is a, this is a very traditional, um, shmup, but it's also, it's also like the weirdest shmup we've ever played.

00:05:30.040 --> 00:05:31.360
<v Mike>And we played some weird ones.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:33.000
<v Joe>Yeah. Yeah.

00:05:33.100 --> 00:05:34.200
<v Joe>It feels different.

00:05:34.360 --> 00:05:36.820
<v Joe>It feels very different than the way other shmups control.

00:05:37.560 --> 00:05:46.840
<v Mike>Yeah. And it's like very chaotic to look at because again, the destruction of the buildings is, I mean, like, look in, in some respects, the game is pretty metal too.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:48.700
<v Mike>Like this is a pretty sick game.

00:05:49.320 --> 00:05:59.560
<v Mike>And, uh, I could definitely see, you know, you blast some, uh, some Metallica behind this and, uh, you know, with the, with like a bag of Doritos, this could be a pretty good time.

00:05:59.560 --> 00:06:07.260
<v Mike>But, um, but like it, it makes it all like harder to read and harder to actually play, but maybe that's not even the goal here.

00:06:07.400 --> 00:06:18.480
<v Mike>Is this just like, is this just supposed to be like a, a visually intense game with a ridiculous plot and everything is just so absurd that like, this is the natural conclusion for video games?

00:06:19.840 --> 00:06:21.760
<v Sean>Yeah. I mean, I think, I don't know.

00:06:21.940 --> 00:06:23.140
<v Joe>I, I, I, I don't know.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:28.260
<v Joe>I cannot get into the heads of these developers, but I mean, yeah, I, I do agree with you.

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:43.740
<v Joe>I mean, just looking at this, some, some very beautiful like design work with like, I am looking at right now, the second, the second stage where you got like the dark clouds up above and you've got this, like, what looks like some kind of military facility below mountains in the background.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:49.460
<v Joe>And like, kind of like the, the, the remnants of a sunset course sort of on the horizon.

00:06:49.460 --> 00:06:53.440
<v Joe>Like it's very, it looks very intense.

00:06:53.440 --> 00:06:54.380
<v Mike>Yeah.

00:06:54.600 --> 00:07:07.180
<v Mike>And, uh, the game itself developed by, uh, I think it's pronounced Kaze, uh, which was headed up by a, uh, a former record firm turned game publisher.

00:07:08.040 --> 00:07:12.660
<v Mike>And, uh, the head of development was previously a composer at Namco.

00:07:12.900 --> 00:07:22.180
<v Mike>So I guess that's how the record firm works in, but it's basically like a bunch of music people who I guess have the ability to program wind up making, uh, this game.

00:07:22.180 --> 00:07:31.500
<v Mike>And I don't know if this is just like a clerical error or whatever, but the studio was founded one month before the Japanese release of this game.

00:07:31.640 --> 00:07:41.700
<v Mike>So I don't know if that means that they like were working on it for a little bit and then incorporated the company, but it, it, it'd be, it, I don't think it's out of line to say that this game could be made in a month.

00:07:42.080 --> 00:07:42.980
<v Sean>Yeah. I don't know.

00:07:43.100 --> 00:07:50.100
<v Joe>I don't know how these timelines, uh, worked, but yeah, it seems like this is like, there's, there's some weirdness in this game for sure.

00:07:50.200 --> 00:07:51.240
<v Joe>Some jank for sure.

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:56.980
<v Sean>But all that aside, like this is, it's a pretty robust, at least design.

00:07:57.760 --> 00:08:02.720
<v Joe>And like, there's enough in it where like, yeah, I don't think this was like, oh yeah, we just pumped this out like real quick.

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:20.640
<v Mike>And, uh, once again, you are just a head, not a character with a head, not a character who lost their head, a giant floating orange samurai head drifting through the skies above American cities, firing like those eyeballs.

00:08:20.920 --> 00:08:22.720
<v Mike>Like what is it? Fireballs or eyeballs?

00:08:22.860 --> 00:08:24.720
<v Mike>Cause they're coming out of his eyes.

00:08:25.160 --> 00:08:29.560
<v Joe>Oh, I saw, I thought that they were fireballs, but you have two projectiles.

00:08:29.700 --> 00:08:38.120
<v Joe>There's, yeah, there's like one coming out of your eyes and they kind of look to me like the, uh, fire flower fireballs, but like they don't bounce.

00:08:38.280 --> 00:08:42.840
<v Joe>They just like go up and then you're like vomiting flames out of your mouth that go down.

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:45.000
<v Mike>Um, well, the, yeah, and that's helpful, right?

00:08:45.060 --> 00:08:47.800
<v Mike>Like the ones that, the ones coming out of your eyes, like shoot across.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:52.440
<v Mike>And then you have the ones shooting out of your mouth, which, um, drop down to the ground.

00:08:52.440 --> 00:08:56.740
<v Mike>So this way you can attack both like things below you and on the same plane as you.

00:08:56.840 --> 00:08:59.800
<v Mike>So I, I think that that is a good call to have.

00:08:59.980 --> 00:09:03.820
<v Mike>It is a messy call though, because it's all like the same color sprite.

00:09:03.820 --> 00:09:09.760
<v Mike>And so like, yeah, there is some like subtle differences, but it is just, it's just like a mess to read this game.

00:09:09.760 --> 00:09:23.680
<v Mike>And I, I know I've said that, but the reason why I brought up the head again is because apparently there is a connection to a historical, um, real samurai who's, uh, and I don't, I don't know how to pronounce the name.

00:09:23.800 --> 00:09:36.220
<v Mike>It looks like it's Taira no Masakado, who's a real samurai whose decapitated head is said to have become a vengeful spirit searching for its body after it was buried separately from its head.

00:09:37.000 --> 00:09:44.240
<v Mike>And that is like what this character is based after all, all this stuff is lost on the American audience though.

00:09:44.360 --> 00:09:45.560
<v Mike>Right? Yeah.

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:49.280
<v Joe>That's why like when I booted this up, I was like, Oh, I definitely am missing some context.

00:09:49.420 --> 00:09:52.400
<v Joe>It feels like they just expected to me to know that this was going to be happening.

00:09:53.060 --> 00:09:56.800
<v Joe>Um, so I just was like, you know, I'm along for the ride.

00:09:57.340 --> 00:09:58.900
<v Joe>Let's, let's do it. We're floating head.

00:09:58.900 --> 00:10:00.540
<v Joe>We shoot flare out of our eyes and mouth.

00:10:00.980 --> 00:10:02.780
<v Mike>And here's what, here's where it gets weirder.

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:07.740
<v Mike>The Japanese version of the game, the original version didn't have the samurai head.

00:10:07.900 --> 00:10:12.580
<v Mike>Instead, it was a giant floating Tengu mask from like Japanese folklore.

00:10:13.060 --> 00:10:19.340
<v Mike>And so you don't even have that samurai connection, you know, to the original game or whatever.

00:10:19.460 --> 00:10:21.100
<v Mike>It's not like, Oh, it's just poorly translated.

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:24.180
<v Mike>Like they had the opportunity to change this to anything else.

00:10:24.300 --> 00:10:25.780
<v Mike>And they chose floating head.

00:10:26.180 --> 00:10:32.420
<v Joe>Do you think that they'd like picked this head because it, because like you said, it looks so metal and they were just like, yeah, American audiences.

00:10:32.420 --> 00:10:34.500
<v Joe>They want like, they want grunge.

00:10:34.780 --> 00:10:38.320
<v Joe>They want look something that looks like, you know, mean.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:41.280
<v Mike>Yeah. Cause it does look like his head's been like freshly ripped off too.

00:10:41.360 --> 00:10:45.120
<v Mike>Like you still see the, uh, the neck and like he's unshaven and stuff.

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:46.440
<v Sean>Like, but like, he's not hurt.

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:47.300
<v Sean>He's pissed.

00:10:47.800 --> 00:10:48.960
<v Sean>Yeah. And he's scary.

00:10:48.960 --> 00:10:50.240
<v Mike>Right. You'd be pissed too.

00:10:50.380 --> 00:10:53.100
<v Mike>If you were a giant head disturbed from your slumber.

00:10:53.100 --> 00:10:59.580
<v Joe>Sure. Um, but I, yeah, I do want to just touch on what you said about the, uh, about the projectiles looking messy.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:09.820
<v Joe>I thought that was one of the many things that made, I think like the best way I can describe the feeling of actually playing this game with the way you're shooting things and the way enemies are coming out of you is chaos.

00:11:10.660 --> 00:11:12.720
<v Joe>Because like I, it is hard to know what's going on.

00:11:12.780 --> 00:11:15.620
<v Joe>I mean, even the enemies are kind of like the same color as your projectile.

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.740
<v Joe>It's just like this spew of orange and red across the cityscape.

00:11:21.160 --> 00:11:24.180
<v Mike>Yeah. And, and everything is always animating too.

00:11:24.300 --> 00:11:30.440
<v Mike>So it's even like if you destroyed the building and then the building like fell or something, so it was out of the way, that would probably be better here.

00:11:30.580 --> 00:11:34.660
<v Mike>But instead, awesomely so it just stays on fire.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:44.380
<v Mike>And now you just have to like, like, you don't get a, you don't get hurt by it, but you have to like, just deal with the consequences of like more animation on your screen and trying to like read through it.

00:11:46.020 --> 00:11:48.820
<v Joe>Yeah. Yeah. I mean, it's, it's, it's, it's a mess.

00:11:49.100 --> 00:11:53.160
<v Mike>I just don't see how this is helping the zombie, uh, problem.

00:11:53.420 --> 00:12:00.860
<v Mike>Like I don't see how destroying all this, all these cities helps us in the, you know, like in the canon of the universe.

00:12:00.860 --> 00:12:07.240
<v Joe>That's why I thought that like the, the word zombie was just in there to justify like we're killing.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:10.560
<v Joe>Cause like right now it's like, okay, it's like we're fighting the military.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:14.740
<v Joe>We're attacking the city and fighting all the people in the city.

00:12:15.060 --> 00:12:22.820
<v Joe>It seems like we're the bad guy, unless all the people in the world or in the United States are the bad guys because they're zombies.

00:12:22.820 --> 00:12:31.780
<v Joe>zombies. So it's, yeah, I don't know what you do once you've killed all the zombies and there's just nobody left, but maybe you just need to kill all the zombies in order to get to the, to the final threat.

00:12:32.000 --> 00:12:38.880
<v Mike>And as you're destroying all these buildings, occasionally a person will like fall out of them and start screaming for help.

